===Etymology===
From {{der|en|fr|épidémique}}, from {{m|fr|épidémie}}, from {{der|en|ML.|epidēmia}}, reanalysis of plural {{der|en|LL.|epidēmia}}, from {{der|en|grc|ἐπιδήμιος}}, from {{m|grc|ἐπί|t=upon}} + {{m|grc|δῆμος|t=people}}. {{surf|en|epi-|t1=on|demic|t2=of the people}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɛpɪˈdɛmɪk/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-epidemic.ogg}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛmɪk|s=4}}
* {{hyph|en|ep|i|dem|ic}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[widespread]] [[disease]] that [[affect]]s many [[human]]s in a [[population]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1950|month=January|title=Notes and News: George Bradshaw's Grave|journal=Railway Magazine|pages=61–62|text=At that time, the city [Christiania, now Oslo] was in the grip of a cholera '''epidemic''', and victims were dying at the rate of 60 a day. Bradshaw contracted the disease, and died on September 6 [1853].}}
# {{lb|en|epidemiology}} An [[occurrence]] of a disease or [[disorder]] in a human population at a [[frequency]] higher than that expected in a given time period; an [[episode]] of [[outbreak]] and subsequent high [[prevalence]].
#: {{cot|en|q=epi-|epizootic#Noun<qq:in nonhuman animals>|epiphytotic#Noun<qq:in plants>|;|q3=en-|endemic#Noun|enzootic#Noun|enphytotic#Noun}}
# {{lb|en|by extension|colloquial}} A heightened occurrence of anything harmful.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=John Leland|quotee=Vivek Murthy|title=How Loneliness Is Damaging Our Health|work=The New York Times|date=2022-04-20|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/04/20/nyregion/loneliness-epidemic.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=Even before the pandemic, the United States surgeon general, Vivek Murthy, said the country was experiencing an “'''epidemic''' of loneliness,” driven by the accelerated pace of life and the spread of technology into all of our social interactions.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en-GB|author=Laura Laker|title=UK protests planned amid ‘epidemic’ of dangerous driving|work=The Guardian|date=2023-09-29|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/sep/29/uk-protests-planned-amid-epidemic-of-dangerous-driving|issn=0261-3077|passage=People are holding coordinated protests across UK towns and cities this weekend against what they call a “climate of fear” on the roads, and an “'''epidemic'''” of careless and dangerous driving that is curbing children’s freedoms and putting lives at risk.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} The spreading of an [[idea]] or [[belief]] amongst a [[population]].
#* {{RQ:Landon Francesca Carrara|pages=98–99|volume=III|text=Lord Avonleigh was at once liberated from his imprisonment, well prepared to be considered, and to consider himself, a martyr to the cause of loyalty; and as the services of the rich nobleman,...his claims to notice and favour were most graciously acknowledged. Accordingly, he returned to his seat in a little fever of royal devotedness—it was the fashionable '''epidemic'''; and who coming from Whitehall could be without it?}}

