===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Of, related to, or being an [[#Noun|epidemic]]: a widespread outbreak of disease in humans.
#: {{cot|en|q=epi-|epizootic#Adjective<qq:in nonhuman animals>|epiphytotic#Adjective<qq:in plants>|;|q3=en-|endemic#Adjective|enzootic#Adjective|enphytotic#Adjective|;|q6=pan-|pandemic#Adjective|panzootic#Adjective}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} Like an [[#Noun|epidemic]]: [[widespread]] in a bad way.
#: {{syn|en|common|ubiquitous|Thesaurus:widespread}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Epidemic''' hysteria occurred upon the incumbent’s reelection.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1852|title=Annals of influenza or epidemic catarrhal fever in Great Britain|page=76|passage=[In] May, there was, at London and in its neighbourhood, a disease very '''epidemic''', though not fatal, which had some time before been very prevalent both in Italy and Germany.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1986|author=Gerald F. Pyle|title=The Diffusion of Influenza: Patterns and Paradigms|page=123|isbn=0847674290|passage=The major reason for such an examination was to determine if any patterns uncovered seemed to be more '''epidemic''' than endemic.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=James C. Howell|title=Preventing & Reducing Juvenile Delinquency: A Comprehensive Framework|publisher=SAGE Publications|ISBN=978-0-7619-2509-5|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=kICmv_FbUjcC&pg=PA19&dq=epidemic|page=19|passage=This proportion increased about 5% from 1988 to 1992—hardly a change of '''epidemic''' proportions.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Frederick Allen|title=A Decent, Orderly Lynching: The Montana Vigilantes|page=8|passage=This was the stagecoach holdup, and while these encounters were not as '''epidemic''' as we like to remember, nonetheless there were numerous bands of "road agents" who lay by the roadside in wait for passengers.}}

